\nFrom shared dossier and our private notes:\n\nCandidate A: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Private: regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas. So strengths: technical competence (complex connections, spatial vision), leadership (good). Concerns: attitude (show-off, not open to new ideas) may affect crew cooperation, adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Private: weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability (conscientious), stress resilience, weather assessment. Concerns: memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), professional communication (nasty remarks) and crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Private: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: communication, teamwork, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Private: regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Also no info on crew cooperation or professional communication. Strengths: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, adaptability to unexpected events. Concerns: attitude (know-it-all, hot temper) may affect crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to decide provisional vote. Which candidate best fits overall? All have some concerns. Candidate B has strong reliability and stress handling, but communication issues. Candidate C strong decision and calm but egocentric and poor communication. Candidate D strong responsibility and problem solving but hot temper and know-it-all. Candidate A strong technical and leadership but show-off and not open to ideas.\n\nWhich is best? For long-distance pilot, reliability, stress, technical competence, decision quality, crew cooperation are critical. Communication and teamwork are essential. Candidate B's nasty remarks are serious for crew cooperation. Candidate C's egocentric and communication difficulty also problematic. Candidate D's hot temper also problematic. Candidate A's show-off and not open to ideas may affect cooperation but perhaps less severe? Show-off could affect crew dynamics. Not open to new ideas could affect adaptability.\n\nOverall, maybe Candidate A has strongest technical competence and leadership, but attitude concerns. Candidate B has reliability but communication issues. Which is less severe? Probably communication issues (nasty remarks) are more safety-critical than being a show-off. So maybe Candidate A is preferable.\n\nThus provisional vote: Candidate A.\n\nNow we need to share some evidence to support that and note unresolved issues (e.g., attitude). Also note that we have no info on D's crew cooperation, which is missing.\n\nPublic message: share our private observations about Candidate A being regarded as a show-off and not open to new ideas, which raises concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability. Also note that Candidate B's nasty remarks are concerning. Provide a brief comparison and state provisional vote for Candidate A, pending further input on crew cooperation for D.\n\nLet's craft concise message."}
